14. DEPENDENCY-AWARE INCREMENTAL RE-SCRUTINY

Implement a demonstrable prototype of this proposed feature.

Goal

Reduce unnecessary repeated checks when evidence or applicable regulatory rules change.

Data model

Every verification check should store:

- Check ID
- Application ID
- Rule ID and version
- Input evidence IDs and versions
- Relevant configuration version
- Dependency references
- Previous result
- Execution timestamp
- Execution status
- Result explanation

Processing algorithm

When evidence changes:

1. Identify the changed evidence ID and version.
2. Find checks that depend on the changed evidence.
3. Expand dependencies to identify downstream affected checks.
4. Rerun affected checks only if the dependency map is complete and all relevant inputs can be validated.
5. Preserve unaffected prior results only when all their evidence versions, rule versions, dependency references, and relevant configuration remain unchanged.
6. If any dependency is missing, uncertain, or inconsistent, fall back to full revalidation.
7. Store the new results.
8. Record which checks were rerun and why.
9. Show the change in the scrutiny history.

When a rule version changes, identify all checks that depend on that rule or affected requirements.

User interface

Add a button:

“Analyze Impact of Changes”

The results panel should display:

- Changed evidence or rule
- Affected requirements
- Affected checks
- Unaffected checks
- Checks requiring full revalidation
- Execution status
- Previous and current results

For demo purposes, create deterministic test cases showing both incremental and full revalidation paths.

Do not claim a measured efficiency improvement until the feature has been benchmarked.

